# The shell profile for a bare-metal InferNode.
#
# NOT lib/sh/profile from the hosted tree. That one mounts a host
# filesystem, starts mntgen, and wires up telephony -- none of which
# exists here, and each failure would print at a user who has just
# booted a machine and cannot yet tell a missing feature from a broken
# one. Name service is started below, with ndb/dns, which the hosted
# tree does without because its host resolves names for it.
#
# sh reads this only when started with -l, which osinit does.

# Control flow lives in loadable modules under /dis/sh, not in sh
# itself, so without this a shell can run commands and pipelines but has
# no if, while or for at all -- "for(i in 1 2 3)" fails looking for
# ./for.
load std

# The default is already (/dis .) -- see sh.b -- and it is set here so
# that it is stated somewhere a person can read and change, rather than
# being a constant compiled into the shell.
path=(/dis .)

# The secstore daemon, when the userspace provides one. The kernel
# image does not carry it; a card or network root grown by the
# rootpath policy does (dis/auth/secstored.dis), and wm/logon's unlock
# dials it on localhost. Errors are dropped deliberately: on a machine
# running only the built-in root this line must be silence, not a
# complaint about a daemon the image never promised.
auth/secstored &

# Names. ndb/dns asks the name servers the network named in /net/ndb
# (the Ethernet driver's DHCP, or ip/dhcp's), and ndb/cs translates
# the names dial is given. Without them every name fails at once with
# "invalid IP address": a browser shows nothing for any page. They come
# from the card, so errors are dropped as secstored's are.
ndb/dns -r >[2] /dev/null
ndb/cs >[2] /dev/null

# The desktop, when the userspace provides it. Backgrounded for the
# same reason secstored's errors are dropped: this console must come
# up as a shell no matter what the card holds, and the GUI belongs to
# the framebuffer, not to the serial line.
# stderr stays on the console: a dying logon must be SEEN. The
# no-userspace case prints one open error, which is honest.
sh /lib/lucifer/boot-baremetal.sh >[2] /dev/cons &
